Actually, wait. Let me reconsider. In `tacacs_decode_args`, there's the `raw:` label:

```c
da = fr_dict_attr_by_name(NULL, fr_dict_root(dict_tacacs), (char *) buffer);
if (!da) {
raw:
    da = parent;
    value = p;
    arg_end = p + arg_list[i];
}
```

So when `da` is not found, `value = p` and `arg_end = p + arg_list[i]`. Here `p` points into the input buffer (the packet data), NOT into `buffer[256]`. So the value string is `[p, p + arg_list[i])` in the packet data.

But wait, this is the `raw` path. In this case, `da = parent = attr_tacacs_argument_list` which is `FR_TYPE_STRING`. So `fr_pair_value_from_str` would parse it as a STRING, not OCTETS. So no base16 decode.

Hmm. Let me reconsider. The `fr_pair_value_from_str` is called with `value` and `arg_end - value`. In the normal path (da found), `value` points into `buffer[256]`. In the raw path, `value = p` points into the packet.

Wait, but in the normal path, after finding `da`, the code does:
```c
if (fr_pair_value_from_str(vp, (char const *) value, arg_end - value, 0, true) < 0) {
    talloc_free(vp);
    if (da != parent) goto raw;
    goto next;
}
```

So if parsing fails and `da != parent`, it goes to `raw`, which sets `value = p` (the packet data) and `arg_end = p + arg_list[i]`, and `da = parent` (STRING type). Then it re-parses as STRING.

So the base16 decode happens in the normal path where `value` points into `buffer[256]`.

OK so let me reconsider. The value string is `[value, arg_end)` within `buffer[256]`. For OCTETS with "0x" prefix, `fr_base16_decode` reads within `[value+2, arg_end)`. This is within `buffer[256]`.

